00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.600
But there's no denying that they're getting better at it

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.000
and Google, like it or not,

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.400
has in-depth analytical access to the

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.900
world's largest database of recorded, spoken words.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.500
Which is YouTube.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.100
Kinda strange to think of it that way, but

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.900
that is one of the things that YouTube is.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.800
It also has a very deep well of talent;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.200
very deep pockets

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.300
and strong economic incentives for being able to

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.300
figure out the words that are coming outta my mouth.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.100
Go to find the lady that held the brotherhood's web servers.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.200
If they could figure out what I'm talking about

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.500
they could run some ads down here,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.300
and over here.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.900
(ex') talking about exactly the stuff

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:29.700
that I'm talking about

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.500
This 'contextual ad' thing is how Google makes

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.100
all of its money, so they would like to

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.200
be able to do that with video.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.200
They also want us to be able to talk into our phones,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.200
which I can do right now

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.200
"Google is sometimes kind of creepy"

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:40.900
*beep*

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.800
Google is the first kind of creepy.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.200
And that's pretty amazing.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.000
But there are broader implications of this technology

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.700
that *wuaaah* kinda creep me out.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:52.900
Unfortunately for Google, undertstanding

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:54.500
what words are being said and understanding

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.000
what those words mean, when strung

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.100
together into a sentence, they're

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.300
completely different tasks.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.900
And one of them, which we're talking about right now,

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:00.800
is quite difficult.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.800
But understanding the meaning of the sentence

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.900
turns out to be even harder.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.800
Google has very strong economic incentives

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.400
to figure out what sentences mean,

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.400
and so far, they've been profoundly unable to do that.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.700
Even with those deep pockets and all that talent

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.800
Google is completely unable to turn a sentence,

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.200
like the one I'm saying right now,

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.500
into useful information.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.400
The software would be required to do far

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.000
too much analysis and synthesis of information

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.600
on its own.  And there's so much cultural context

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:29.800
that goes into all the stuff that we say,

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.100
and the tone of our voice.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:32.500
If a program could actually understand

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.000
and synthesize data from these complex sentences,

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.300
that would pretty much be artificial intelligence.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.000
I think that we dramatically underestimated

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.400
the complexity of our own brains.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.400
And artificial intelligence would require us

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:44.500
to write the software that runs our brains,

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.000
and we have NO idea how our brains work.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.900
But that does not mean that 100% accurate

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.100
speech recognition would not have

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.800
HUGE implications for the world.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.600
If speech recognition were 100% accurate

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.100
for two different languages

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.700
subtitling conversations using handheld devices,

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.800
or even in-ear or in-eye devices,

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:01.000
would become trivial,

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.400
except that the translations would, of course,

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.200
be machine based.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.500
And that's not entirely accurate, as anyone

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.400
who has used Google Translate can tell you,

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.800
but combining speech recognition

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.200
with an actual bionic implant, of a kind,

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.900
(which doesn't actually, to me, seem all that far off)

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.500
would, I think, have really dramatic implications

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.500
on what it means to be a person.  And anybody

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:19.400
who hasn't thought about that

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.200
should read this book.  It's called Feed.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.200
So yes.  These funny little snippets of wrongly

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.200
translated text are funny.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.900
They're cute, they're bad, and we talk

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:28.500
way too fast for them to understand us.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.400
But that is just right now.  And if we know

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.000
anything about how technology works

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.600
it probably won't be too long before they

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:34.200
figure it out.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.600
So it's probably very worth,

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.900
